+# Privacy by design — The Daily Grind website
+
+This file records how the site keeps visitor data to a minimum and why each control
+exists. Update it whenever you add a script, embed, form field, cookie or header.
+
+## 1. Data inventory (data minimization)
+
+| Data | Collected? | Where | Purpose | Retention |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Session identifier | Yes (signed-in users) | `__Host-session` cookie, HttpOnly | Keep the user signed in | Session; cleared on logout |
+| Web storage (`localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB) | **No** | — | — | Wiped on logout anyway |
+| Analytics / advertising IDs | **No** | — | — | — |
+| IP address / URLs in client logs | **No** | `server.mjs` logs nothing per request | — | — |
+| Referrer sent to other sites | **No** | `Referrer-Policy: same-origin` | — | — |
+
+Rules:
+- Don't collect data "just in case". Each new form field needs a purpose, an inline
+  "why we ask" note next to it, and a retention period listed in this table.
+- Ask for the coarsest value that works (e.g. an age bracket, not a date of birth;
+  a postcode area, not a GPS position).
+- Users can access, export, correct and delete their data at `/account/privacy`, linked
+  from every page footer. Deleting data must be self-serve, like sign-up.
+
+## 2. Third-party audit
+
+Last audited: 2026-11-04. Method: review of every URL in `index.html`, `css/` and `js/`,
+plus the CSP. `default-src 'none'` and per-type `'self'` mean the browser blocks any
+third-party request that was missed.
+
+| Third party | Before | Now | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash / imgix (hero image) | Loaded from `images.unsplash.com` on every visit, exposing visitor IPs and the referring page | **Removed.** Self-hosted at `/images/hero.jpg` (Unsplash License), checked to contain no EXIF/GPS metadata | A stock photo doesn't need a third-party request |
+| `statikk` via `pnpm dlx` (dev server) | Unpinned package downloaded and run on every `npm start` | **Removed.** Replaced by dependency-free `server.mjs` | Supply-chain risk; it also couldn't send our headers |
+| Web fonts | None (system font stack) | None | — |
+| Analytics, tag managers, pixels, social SDKs, chat widgets | None | None, and blocked by the CSP | — |
+
+Before adding any third party:
+1. Check whether a first-party or static alternative exists (for example, plain share
+   links instead of social SDKs, or a click-to-load facade with `youtube-nocookie.com`
+   instead of an auto-loading embed).
+2. Record a HAR or a DevTools Network capture and list exactly what the third party
+   receives (cookies, referrer, identifiers).
+3. Pin a versioned URL and add `integrity`, `crossorigin="anonymous"` and
+   `referrerpolicy="no-referrer"`.
+4. Widen only the specific CSP directive needed, on only the pages that need it. Keep
+   it off checkout and account pages unless it's strictly required.
+5. Add a row to the table above.
+
+## 3. Response headers (`_headers`)
+
+`_headers` is the single source of truth. Netlify and Cloudflare Pages read it
+natively, and `server.mjs` applies the same file locally. For other hosts, copy the
+values into the host's config. `index.html` carries `<meta>` fallbacks for the CSP and
+referrer policy; keep them in sync.
+
+| Header | Value (summary) | Privacy / security effect |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `Content-Security-Policy` | `default-src 'none'`; `'self'` for scripts, styles and images; `form-action 'self'`; `frame-ancestors 'none'`; `base-uri 'none'`; `object-src 'none'`; Trusted Types with no policies | Blocks third-party loads, data exfiltration via `fetch`/beacons (`connect-src` falls back to `'none'`), clickjacking and DOM XSS. No inline code is allowed: CSS and JS are external files |
+| `Referrer-Policy` | `same-origin` | No URL or origin is leaked to other sites |
+| `Permissions-Policy` | Every powerful feature `=()`, plus the ad/tracking APIs (`browsing-topics`, `join-ad-interest-group`, `run-ad-auction`, `attribution-reporting`, private state tokens) | Neither the page nor any iframe can request the camera, location, sensors and so on, or join ad-tech APIs. Relax per page only when needed (e.g. `geolocation=(self)` on `/locations`, `publickey-credentials-get=(self)` for passkeys on `/account`) |
+| `Strict-Transport-Security` | 1 year, `includeSubDomains` | Forces HTTPS. For a first rollout, start with `max-age=300` and ramp up |
+| `X-Content-Type-Options` | `nosniff` | Blocks MIME sniffing |
+| `X-Frame-Options` | `DENY` | Clickjacking protection for older browsers |
+| `X-DNS-Prefetch-Control` | `off` | No speculative DNS lookups that reveal which links are on the page |
+| `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` | `same-origin-allow-popups` | Isolates the window from malicious openers (XS-Leaks) while still allowing payment/OAuth popups |
+| `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` | `same-origin` (`cross-origin` for `gpc.json`) | Other sites can't embed or read our resources |
+| `Origin-Agent-Cluster` | `?1` | Process isolation per origin |
+| `Clear-Site-Data` (on `/logout`) | `"cache", "cookies", "storage"` | Leaves nothing behind on shared devices. `js/privacy.js` also clears web storage as a fallback |
+| `Cache-Control: no-store` (on `/logout` and `/account*`) | — | Personal pages are never written to disk or shared caches |
+
+Deliberately not set: `Reporting-Endpoints` / `report-to`. Violation reports contain
+URLs and can contain user data. Add them only with a first-party collector that strips
+query strings and IPs at the edge.
+
+## 4. Requirements for the backend
+
+The static site depends on the backend for these:
+
+- **Session cookie:** `Set-Cookie: __Host-session=…; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`.
+  No `Domain` attribute, no unpartitioned `SameSite=None`, never readable by JS.
+- **Logout:** accept `POST /logout` only (the nav button is now a form, so link
+  prefetching, crawlers and cross-site `<img>` tags can't log users out). Destroy the
+  server-side session, then respond with the `/logout` headers above and a `303` to `/`.
+- **Logs:** mask emails, tokens and IDs; don't log full query strings or IP addresses
+  beyond what abuse prevention needs, and set a retention limit.
+- **Rate limiting:** apply it to account and lookup endpoints to prevent bulk scraping.
+- **Global Privacy Control:** treat the `Sec-GPC: 1` request header as an opt-out of
+  sale/sharing. Support is declared in `/.well-known/gpc.json`.
+
+## 5. Client-side script rules (`js/privacy.js`)
+
+- First-party scripts only, loaded with `defer`. No inline handlers.
+- No network calls from script, no storage of PII or tokens, no `innerHTML`-style
+  sinks (Trusted Types enforces this), and feature detection instead of user-agent
+  sniffing or fingerprinting.
+- The script currently does two things: it clears web storage when the logout form is
+  submitted, and it shows a notice confirming we honor GPC when
+  `navigator.globalPrivacyControl` is on.
